《Biographies of Zhang Shizhi and Feng Tang》

Zhang Tingwei Shizhi was a native of Duyang, with the courtesy name Ji. He had an elder brother, Zhong, with whom he lived. He became a Cavalier Attendant by virtue of his family's wealth and served Emperor Xiaowen. For ten years he was not promoted and gained no fame. Shizhi said, "My long service has depleted Zhong's property, and I have not achieved success." He wished to resign and return home. Zhonglangjiang Yuan Ang knew he was worthy and regretted his departure, so he requested that Shizhi be transferred to fill the post of Yezhe. After Shizhi completed the court audience, he approached the throne to discuss matters of convenience. Emperor Wen said, "Keep it low; do not make high-flown arguments, so that it can be implemented today." Thereupon, Shizhi spoke of matters between the Qin and Han dynasties, and the reasons why Qin lost and Han rose, for a long time. Emperor Wen praised him and appointed Shizhi as Chief of the Yezhe.

Shizhi accompanied the Emperor on an excursion and climbed the Tiger Enclosure. The Emperor asked the Shanglin Commandant about the registers of birds and beasts. He asked more than ten questions, but the Commandant looked left and right and could not answer any. The Sefu of the Tiger Enclosure stood by and answered the Emperor's questions about the registers of birds and beasts very thoroughly, wishing to display his ability to respond fluently and endlessly. Emperor Wen said, "Should officials not be like this? The Commandant is useless!" He then ordered Shizhi to appoint the Sefu as the Magistrate of Shanglin. Shizhi approached after a while and said, "Your Majesty, what kind of person do you consider Marquis of Jiang, Zhou Bo, to be?" The Emperor said, "An elder." He asked again, "What kind of person do you consider Marquis of Dongyang, Zhang Xiangru, to be?" The Emperor again said, "An elder." Shizhi said, "The Marquis of Jiang and the Marquis of Dongyang are called elders, yet these two men could not even get words out of their mouths when speaking on matters. How could they emulate this Sefu who chatters rapidly and speaks with quick wit? Moreover, the Qin employed clerks who wielded brushes, and officials competed to excel in harsh scrutiny. However, the drawback was that it was merely formalistic, lacking the substance of compassion. Consequently, their faults were not heard, and they declined until the Second Emperor, when the realm collapsed like earth. Now Your Majesty promotes the Sefu rapidly because of his eloquence. I fear the realm will follow the wind, competing in eloquence without substance. Moreover, the influence of superiors on subordinates is swifter than shadows and echoes. Actions and decisions must be examined carefully." Emperor Wen said, "Good." He then stopped and did not appoint the Sefu.

The Emperor boarded his carriage, summoned Shizhi to ride beside him, and proceeded slowly. He asked Shizhi about the abuses of the Qin. Shizhi spoke frankly and in detail. Upon arriving at the palace, the Emperor appointed Shizhi as the Magistrate of the Public Carriage Office.

After a while, the Crown Prince and the King of Liang entered the court in the same carriage. They did not dismount at the Sima Gate. Thereupon, Shizhi chased and stopped the Crown Prince and the King of Liang, preventing them from entering the Hall Gate. He then impeached them for disrespect for not dismounting at the public gate and submitted a memorial. Empress Dowager Bo heard of this. Emperor Wen removed his cap and apologized, saying, "I have been negligent in teaching my sons." Empress Dowager Bo then sent an envoy to convey her edict pardoning the Crown Prince and the King of Liang, after which they were allowed to enter. Emperor Wen thereafter considered Shizhi extraordinary and appointed him as Zhongdafu.

After a while, he rose to the rank of Zhonglangjiang. Accompanying the Emperor to Baling, they stood on the north side overlooking the toilet. At that time, Lady Shen accompanied them. The Emperor pointed out the road to Xinfeng to Lady Shen, saying, "This is the road to Handan." He had Lady Shen play the se. The Emperor himself sang while leaning on the se, his mind filled with sorrow and melancholy. He turned to his ministers and said, "Alas! If I use stone from the Northern Mountain for the outer coffin, stuff it with hemp and silk, and fill the gaps with lacquer, can it be moved?" Those around him all said, "Good." Shizhi stepped forward and said, "If there is something desirable within, even if you seal it with the Southern Mountain, there will still be a gap. If there is nothing desirable within, even without a stone coffin, what is there to worry about?" Emperor Wen praised him. Thereafter, he appointed Shizhi as Tingwei.

After a while, the Emperor was traveling out of the Zhongwei Bridge. A man ran out from under the bridge, startling the Emperor's carriage horses. The Emperor sent cavalry to capture him and handed him over to the Tingwei. Shizhi investigated and interrogated him. The man said, "I am a commoner from the county. I heard the imperial guard's warning and hid under the bridge. After a long time, I thought the procession had passed, so I came out. Seeing the imperial carriage and cavalry, I ran." The Tingwei judged the case according to the law: one person violated the imperial guard's warning, and the penalty was a fine. Emperor Wen was angry and said, "This man personally startled my horse. Fortunately, my horse is gentle and docile. If it had been another horse, would I not have been injured? Yet the Tingwei only fines him!" Shizhi said, "The law is what Your Majesty shares with the realm. If the law is as it is, but you increase the penalty, the law will lose credibility with the people. Moreover, at that time, if Your Majesty had ordered him executed on the spot, it would have been over. Now that the case has been handed to the Tingwei, the Tingwei is the balance of the realm. If the balance tilts once, the application of laws throughout the realm will become arbitrary. Where will the people place their hands and feet? I beg Your Majesty to consider this." After a long pause, the Emperor said, "The Tingwei's judgment is correct."

Later, someone stole a jade ring from the front of the Gaozu Temple. He was captured. Emperor Wen was angry and handed the case to the Tingwei for judgment. Shizhi investigated the law: stealing ritual objects and utensils from the ancestral temple warrants a memorial for execution in the marketplace. He submitted the memorial, and the judgment was execution in the marketplace. The Emperor was furious and said, "This man is so depraved that he stole the utensils of the late Emperor's temple. I handed him to the Tingwei because I wanted to exterminate his clan. Yet you judge him according to the law. This is not my intention in reverently serving the ancestral temple." Shizhi removed his cap, bowed his head to the ground, and apologized, saying, "The law is sufficient as it is. Moreover, crimes of the same category are differentiated by the degree of rebellion or compliance. If stealing from the ancestral temple leads to clan extermination, and if there is even a ten-thousandth chance that an ignorant commoner takes a handful of earth from Changling, what law can Your Majesty apply to him?" After a long time, Emperor Wen discussed it with the Empress Dowager, and she agreed with the Tingwei's judgment. At that time, Zhongwei Marquis of Tiao, Zhou Yafu, and the Chancellor of Liang, Marquis of Shandu, Wang Tiankai, saw that Shizhi's judgments were fair, and they formed a close friendship with him. Zhang Tingwei was praised throughout the realm because of this.

Later, Emperor Wen died, and Emperor Jing ascended the throne. Shizhi was afraid and claimed illness. He wished to resign, but feared a great purge; he wished to apologize, but did not know what would happen. Following the advice of Master Wang, he finally went to apologize. Emperor Jing did not blame him.

Master Wang was skilled in Huang-Lao teachings and was a recluse. He was once summoned to sit in the court, where the Three Excellencies and Nine Ministers were all assembled. Master Wang, an old man, said, "My sock is loose," and turned to Zhang Tingwei, saying, "Tie my sock for me!" Shizhi knelt and tied it. Afterward, someone said to Master Wang, "Why did you humiliate Zhang Tingwei in court, making him kneel to tie your sock?" Master Wang said, "I am old and lowly. I estimate that I can no longer benefit Zhang Tingwei. Zhang Tingwei is a famous minister of the realm today. I deliberately humiliated the Tingwei, making him kneel to tie my sock, in order to enhance his reputation." When the nobles heard this, they considered Master Wang worthy and respected Zhang Tingwei even more.

Zhang Tingwei served Emperor Jing for over a year, then became the Chancellor of the King of Huainan, still due to his previous offense. After a long time, Shizhi died. His son was Zhang Zhi, courtesy name Changgong. He reached the rank of Dafu but was dismissed. Because he could not accommodate the world, he never served in office for the rest of his life.

Feng Tang's grandfather was a native of Zhao. His father moved to Dai. When the Han rose, they moved to Anling. Tang was known for his filial piety and served as the Chief of the Zhonglang Office, serving Emperor Wen. When the Emperor passed by in his carriage, he asked Tang, "You are old; why are you a Lang? Where is your home?" Tang answered truthfully in detail. Emperor Wen said, "When I was in Dai, my Food Supervisor, Gao Qu, often spoke to me of the worthiness of the Zhao general Li Qi, who fought at Julu. Now, whenever I eat, my thoughts are always at Julu. Do you know him?" Tang replied, "He is still not as good as Lian Po and Li Mu as generals." The Emperor said, "Why?" Tang said, "My grandfather, while in Zhao, served as a military commander and was on good terms with Li Mu. My father was formerly the Chancellor of Dai and was on good terms with the Zhao general Li Qi, so I know his character." The Emperor, having heard of the characters of Lian Po and Li Mu, was greatly pleased and slapped his thigh, saying, "Alas! If only I had Lian Po and Li Mu as my generals, would I worry about the Xiongnu?" Tang said, "Your servant is bold! Even if Your Majesty had Lian Po and Li Mu, you would not be able to use them." The Emperor was angry, rose, and entered the forbidden palace. After a long time, he summoned Tang and rebuked him, saying, "Why did you humiliate me in front of everyone? Do you have no private place?" Tang apologized, saying, "This rustic does not know taboos."

At that time, the Xiongnu had recently invaded Chaona in large numbers and killed the Commandant of Beidi, Ang. The Emperor was concerned about the Hu raids and finally asked Tang again, "How do you know I cannot use Lian Po and Li Mu?" Tang replied, "I have heard that when ancient kings dispatched generals, they knelt and pushed the wheel of the chariot, saying: 'Within the gate, I control it; outside the gate, the general controls it.' Military merits and rewards were decided outside, and reported upon return. This is not empty talk. My grandfather said that when Li Mu was a Zhao general stationed on the border, the taxes from the military market were all used by him to feast the soldiers. Rewards were decided outside and not interfered with by the court. He was entrusted with responsibility and expected to succeed. Thus, Li Mu was able to exhaust his intelligence and ability. He dispatched 1,300 selected chariots, 13,000 mounted archers, and 100,000 elite soldiers. Therefore, he drove the Chanyu north, defeated the Donghu, destroyed the Danlin, suppressed the powerful Qin to the west, and supported Han and Wei to the south. At that time, Zhao nearly became hegemon. Later, King Qian of Zhao ascended the throne. His mother was a singer. After King Qian ascended, he employed the slander of Guo Kai and finally executed Li Mu, sending Yan Ju to replace him. Consequently, the army was defeated, soldiers fled, and Zhao was captured and destroyed by Qin. Now I have heard that Wei Shang is the Governor of Yunzhong. He uses all the taxes from the military market to feast the soldiers, spends his private funds, slaughters an ox every five days, and feasts guests, military officers, and retainers. Therefore, the Xiongnu stay far away and do not approach the passes of Yunzhong. When the barbarians once entered, Shang led chariots and cavalry to attack them, killing many. The soldiers are all sons of common families, rising from the fields to join the army. How can they know the regulations of the ranks and files? They fight hard all day, beheading enemies and capturing prisoners. When they report their merits to the headquarters, if one word does not match, the civil officials bind them with the law. Their rewards are not granted, but the officials strictly enforce the law. I am foolish, but I think Your Majesty's laws are too strict, rewards too light, and punishments too heavy. Moreover, the Governor of Yunzhong, Wei Shang, was convicted because the number of heads and prisoners he reported differed by six. Your Majesty handed him to the officials, stripped his title, and punished him with labor. From this, I say that even if Your Majesty had Lian Po and Li Mu, you would not be able to use them. I am truly foolish and have touched upon taboos. Death penalty, death penalty!" Emperor Wen was pleased. That day, he ordered Feng Tang to carry the imperial staff to pardon Wei Shang, restore him as Governor of Yunzhong, and appointed Tang as Commandant of Chariots and Cavalry, in charge of the Zhongwei and the chariot soldiers of the commanderies and kingdoms.

In the seventh year, Emperor Jing ascended the throne and appointed Tang as Chancellor of Chu. He was dismissed. When Emperor Wu ascended the throne, he sought worthy and good men, and Feng Tang was recommended. Tang was then over ninety years old and could no longer serve in office. Therefore, Tang's son, Feng Sui, was appointed as a Lang. Sui's courtesy name was Wangsun. He was also an extraordinary man and was on good terms with me.

The Grand Historian says: Zhang Ji's words about elders, upholding the law without flattering intentions; Feng Gong's discussion of generals, how meaningful! How meaningful! The saying goes, "If you do not know a person, look at his friends." The praises of these two men can be inscribed in the temple. The Book of Documents says, "Without bias, without partisanship, the King's Way is broad; without partisanship, without bias, the King's Way is smooth." Zhang Ji and Feng Gong are close to this. [end of text]


